<div id="login">
  <span id="use-password"></span>
  {{{card}}}
  <a href="#use-password">Use a password</a>

  {{> js_setup nodes=nodes}}
  {{#if extraPartial }}
    {{> (extraPartial) }}
  {{/if}}
</div>
{{! The image's own login.hbs with three differences: two of the card's links
    are hidden, its password form is closed until asked for, and its GitHub
    button is filled. The element and the link above are the second, and the
    rest is the rules below, each under its reason. }}
<style>
  {{! The card links sign-up and recovery whether or not kratos.yaml enables
      them. It enables no recovery. It opens registration only to a person who
      signs in with GitHub from a browser holding an invite link, and that is
      the GitHub button on this card. The sign-up link starts a registration by
      hand instead, which kratos.yaml sends to a page of the console that
      registers nobody, so the link stays hidden. }}
  div:has(> a[data-testid="forgot-password-link"]),
  div:has(> div > a[data-testid="signup-link"]) {
    display: none;
  }

  {{! THE CARD OPENS ON GITHUB ALONE. A person new here has no password and
      cannot make one on this card, for the reasons above, and the image draws
      the password form open all the same, which reads as an account to be
      made. An identity that holds a password still signs in with it, so the
      form stays in the card, closed, and the link under the GitHub button
      opens it.

      NO SCRIPT. The link names the empty element before the card, and the
      form is closed only while the page's address does not. Following the
      link also moves where the Tab key starts to that element, so the keys
      after it walk the card from its top.

      A CARD THAT CARRIES A MESSAGE IS DRAWN OPEN. A refused password comes
      back as the same flow with a message, at an address without the
      fragment, and a closed form would hide what the message is about. A
      refresh carries one too, so it is drawn as the image draws it.

      ONLY A CARD THAT OFFERS BOTH IS CLOSED. Without the GitHub button, which
      a refresh leaves out for an identity that holds no GitHub account, a
      closed card would offer nothing but the link that opens it.

      The fields are read by the names Kratos gives them, which a form has to
      post under whatever draws it. The message is read by the image's test
      id, which another image may not carry: a refused password is then still
      told, above a form that is closed. }}
  #login {
    position: relative;
  }
  a[href="#use-password"] {
    display: none;
    position: absolute;
    inset-inline: 0;
    bottom: 1.75rem;
    width: fit-content;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: 0.75rem;
    border-radius: 0.25rem;
    font-family: var(--ory-theme-font-family);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.25rem;
    color: var(--ory-theme-foreground-muted);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
  }
  a[href="#use-password"]:hover {
    color: var(--ory-theme-foreground-def);
  }
  #use-password:not(:target)
    ~ div:has(button[name="provider"]):has(input[name="password"]):not(
      :has([data-testid^="ui/message/"])
    ) {
    {{! The room under the GitHub button that the link is drawn in. }}
    padding-bottom: 5.25rem;
    & form:has(input[name="password"]),
    & form:has(button[name="provider"]) + hr {
      display: none;
    }
    & ~ a[href="#use-password"] {
      display: block;
    }
  }

  {{! The image draws the GitHub button outlined and the password's filled.
      This one is filled in the colours the image gives it while it is
      pressed. The image marks focus on it by the colour of its border, which
      a filled button does not show, so a ring is drawn: around the link, and
      inside the button, because the card's grids clip what is drawn outside
      one. }}
  #login button[name="provider"] {
    color: var(--ory-theme-foreground-on-dark);
    background-color: var(--ory-theme-foreground-def);
    border-color: var(--ory-theme-foreground-def);
  }
  #login button[name="provider"]:hover,
  #login button[name="provider"]:active {
    background-color: var(--ory-theme-foreground-muted);
    border-color: var(--ory-theme-foreground-muted);
  }
  #login button[name="provider"]:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--ory-theme-foreground-on-dark);
    outline-offset: -5px;
  }
  a[href="#use-password"]:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--ory-theme-accent-def);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }
</style>
